Matching-ELF code at `delay_tsc`
-------------------------------
The debug ELF is the matching BuildID `338e542e384c46228f7a378babc0f9bb2b6a5997`
and SHA-256
`7e460f048c3de0f8ee85fd326f2f65d95a6ee448e57b6f5333e2be79b5be9d1f`.
`llvm-nm` resolves the function start as `0xffffffff8271b300`, size `0xbf`.
Disassembly confirms:

* `delay_tsc+0x1b` (`0xffffffff8271b31b`) is the initial `rdtsc`; its value is
  combined and retained as the starting timestamp before control jumps to the
  polling site.
* `delay_tsc+0x4d` (`0xffffffff8271b34d`) is the polling `rdtsc`. The code
  subtracts the starting timestamp, compares elapsed cycles with the requested
  duration, and loops through the `pause`/deadline-check path while the
  duration has not elapsed.

Thus the histogram localizes nearly all of this pair's read-count difference
to repeated polling at `delay_tsc+0x4d`; the entry site differs by 11 hits.
This identifies where the additional reads occurred, not why the number or
length of waits changed. The data do not establish nonce causality or connect
these RDTSC deltas to the separately localized extra page fault. CR3 changes
also remain 26,617/26,618.

The source series' strict comparison remains `matches=false` on
`machine_observations` and nonce-normalized `ordered_uart_bytes`. The histogram
does not change that result or satisfy S5-07 acceptance. Preserve the current
comparator and normalization rules. Astra independently recommended this
offline localization as the next evidence slice; the sidecar arithmetic and
matching-ELF disassembly were checked locally.
